>
> **v2 revision (same day):** scaled the fleet target down from 6/category (24 total) to 4/category (16 total); added Payment/DeliveryRecord/ReturnRecord completeness as an explicit requirement; corrected the root-cause diagnosis of the stale booking window; added two existing-row `status` corrections.
>
> **v3 revision (same day):** v2's 4-per-category split (3 assets sharing one spec-band, 1 asset alone in a second band) was reviewed again and found to have a real gap: the lone 4th asset is a **singleton band** — a band with exactly one asset can only ever report `period_utilization` of `0` or `1`, forever, by construction. That's different from an *empty* band (no data, presumably some defined fallback) — a singleton looks like a real computed value but is structurally incapable of being anything but binary. Since this is hand-picked fixture data, not a real fleet, there's no reason to accept that outcome when every value is a free choice anyway. v3 restructures each category around **one band with all 4 "core" assets (real depth, denominator-4 resolution) plus exactly one asset in every other spec-band** (real, if binary, coverage — not an empty gap that depends on an unverified fallback path elsewhere in Haystack's code). This raises the fleet target from 16 to **27** (see "Outcomes"/`design.md`). Booking, Payment, DeliveryRecord, and ReturnRecord volume are scaled to match. See `design.md` change control for the full diff.

---

## Purpose

`predict_price(...)` (dynamic-pricing spec) reads live `Asset`/`Booking`/`BookingItem`/`AssetCategory` rows to compute `period_utilization`, resolve `capacity`/`platform_height` inputs, and clamp against `Asset.minDailyRate`/`maxDailyRate`. Those code paths are only as verifiable as the data behind them. This spec defines the **shape** the seed data must have — volume, distribution, completeness — for Phase 2's own acceptance scenarios (spec.md US-1 scenarios 4 and 6 in particular) to be checkable against something other than degenerate 0/1 values, and for a demo to show real, differentiated per-asset pricing rather than every asset in a category converging on the same clamped number.

