## Current state (confirmed 2026-08-11, live query against `postgres-haystack`/`heavy_rental`, cross-checked against `src/main/resources/data.sql` in the Spring Boot repo)

| Finding | Detail |
|---|---|
| Fleet depth | 8 assets total, exactly 2 per category (Excavator, Scissors Lift, Boom Lift, Fork Lift) |
| `capacity` | `NULL` on 6 of 8 assets — every asset except the 2 forklifts (which are both `2500`, i.e. identical). Every excavator/scissor-lift/boom-lift prediction today runs through `resolve_effective_capacity()`'s per-category midpoint fallback, never a real per-asset value |
| `condition` spread | Excavator: `GOOD`/`EXCELLENT` only. Fork Lift: `GOOD`/`EXCELLENT` only. Scissors Lift: `EXCELLENT`/`FAIR`. Boom Lift: `GOOD`/`NEEDS_REPAIR`. No category exercises more than 2 of the 4 `ConditionType` values |
| Bookings | 20 rows, single customer (`customer_id=2` on all 20; 4 users exist total, only 1 has `USER` role — `Ravi Kumar`/`Ah Tan` are `ADMIN`/`DRIVER`, not customers) |
| Booking status coverage | Only 4 of 6 `BookingStatus` values appear (`PENDING_CONFIRMED`, `CONFIRMED`, `MOBILISED`, `COMPLETED`). `PENDING_DEPOSIT` never occurs. `CANCELLED` doesn't literally appear either, but booking id 6 is a near-miss — see "Existing-row corrections" |
| Booking window — corrected root cause | Every `start_date`/`end_date` currently falls inside an ~11-day span that looks half-elapsed as of 2026-08-11. This is **not** hardcoded literal dates — `data.sql` already generates every booking date via `CURRENT_DATE ± N` expressions. The apparent staleness is because every booking `INSERT` uses `ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING` (only the `users` block uses `DO UPDATE`), so each date is computed **once**, at whatever moment `data.sql` first ran, then frozen until the DB is dropped. This spec doesn't attempt to fix that underlying mechanism — see "Out of scope" |
| `booking_items` completeness | 10 of 20 bookings (ids 11–20) have **zero** `BookingItem` rows |
| Payment/DeliveryRecord/ReturnRecord completeness | Bookings 11–20 also have no `Payment`, `DeliveryRecord`, or `ReturnRecord` rows — not just `booking_items` |
| `asset_categories.name` values | `Excavator`, `Scissors Lift`, `Boom Lift`, `Fork Lift` — canonical Spring-Boot business names. **Correct as-is; not a data defect** |
| Equipment Browse API scale assumptions | `SPEC-equipment-browse-api.md` justifies two design choices by an assumed asset-count ceiling (per-row lazy-loading of `category` on filtered browse paths; full-size images returned inline on every `browse()` call). As of this v3, that doc's ceiling is updated to ~28 to match — see that doc's own change control |

### Spec-band boundaries (authoritative, from Haystack's `pricing_tables.py`)

```python
CAPACITY_BINS = {
    "excavator": [(0, 3000), (3000, 7000), (7000, 15000), (15000, None)],
    "forklift":  [(0, 2000), (2000, 3500), (3500, None)],
}
HEIGHT_BINS = {
    "scissor lift": [(0, 8), (8, 10), (10, 12), (12, None)],
    "boom lift":    [(0, 18), (18, 24), (24, 31), (31, None)],
}
```

Half-open `(lo, hi]`, except the open-ended last bucket `(lo, None)` which matches anything above `lo`. Excavator/scissor lift/boom lift each have 4 bins; fork lift has 3 (the fleet's real capacity range doesn't reach a 4th industry weight class).

### Existing-row corrections (found during this review, not introduced by this spec)

| Row | Current state | Issue | Fix |
|---|---|---|---|
| `bookings` id 6 | `status='COMPLETED'` | Its only `payments` row is `DEPOSIT`/`FAIL` with a `failure_reason` and no successful payment; its `booking_items` row (id 7) has `NULL` engine-hours/condition fields (the documented pattern for bookings never delivered); no `delivery_records`/`return_records` row. Every associated row already matches what `SPEC-seed-data.md` §6.6 describes as "the one `CANCELLED` booking" | Change `status` to `'CANCELLED'`. No other row changes |
| `bookings` id 2 | `status='MOBILISED'` | Has a `DEPOSIT`+`BALANCE` payment pair summing to the full total, **and** both a `delivery_records` row and a `return_records` row already present. `SPEC-seed-data.md` §6.9/§6.10 describes exactly one `MOBILISED` booking as having a delivery record but no return record yet — that's booking id 4, not id 2 | Change `status` to `'COMPLETED'`. No other row changes |

Neither correction adds or removes a row — both are a single `status` value flip on data that already tells a consistent story once you read across tables.

None of the rest of this is a bug on the Spring Boot side — it's a reasonable minimal fixture that was never sized for a live-aggregate ML feature. This spec is the sizing.

